      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to find the smallest number (n) of coins that satisfies the following two conditions:\n\n1. n ≡ 4 (mod 6)\n2. n ≡ 3 (mod 5)\n\nOnce we find the smallest n that meets these two conditions, we can find the remainder when n is divided by 7.\n\nTo solve this modulo system, we can use the method of successive substitutions or the Chinese Remainder Theorem. Here, I'll use the method of successive substitutions. \n\nWe can start with the first condition, n ≡ 4 (mod 6), and find the remainder when dividing n by 6. Then, we look for a number that leaves a remainder of 4 when divided by 6 and also leaves a remainder of 3 when divided by 5. \n\nFirst, let's find the remainder when n is divided by 6. Since n = 6k + 4 for some integer k, we can plug this into the second condition:\n\n6k + 4 ≡ 3 (mod 5)\n\nNow, we can subtract 4 from both sides:\n\n6k ≡ -1 (mod 5)\n\nSince 6 is congruent to 1 (mod 5), we can substitute this in:\n\nk ≡ -1 (mod 5)\n\nSince -1 is congruent to 4 (mod 5), we have:\n\nk ≡ 4 (mod 5)\n\nNow we know that k and n have the same remainder when divided by 5. We can plug this back into our equation from earlier:\n\nn = 6(4) + 4\n\nn = 24 + 4\n\nn = 28\n\nSo, the smallest number of coins that meets these two conditions is 28. \n\nNow we need to divide these coins evenly among seven people:\n\n28 ÷ 7 = 4, with no remainder\n\nTherefore, the number of coins left when divided among seven people is 0.",
