      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "In the given question, two conditions regarding dividing the coins equally among specified numbers of people are given. Let's represent the number of coins in the box by the variable $n$.\n\nThe first condition states that when the coins are divided among six people, four coins are left over. This can be mathematically expressed as:\n$$ n \\equiv 4 \\pmod{6} \\quad \\quad \\quad (1)$$\n\nThe second condition states that when the coins are divided among five people, three coins are left over. This can be expressed as:\n$$ n \\equiv 3 \\pmod{5} \\quad \\quad \\quad (2)$$\n\nFrom these two conditions, we now look to find the smallest value of $n$ that satisfies both inequalities.\n\nNotice that $n$ minus three should be divisible by the least common multiple (LCM) of six and five. So,\n$$ n = 43 \\text{ (LCM of 6 and 5)} + 3 \\quad \\quad \\quad (3)$$\n\nInserting equation $(3)$ into equation $(1)$, we get:\n$$ n - 3 \\equiv 4 \\pmod{6}$$\n$$ n \\equiv 7 \\pmod{6} \\quad \\quad \\quad (4)$$\n\nComparing equations $(3)$ and $(4)$, we observe that as we increase $n$ by seven, the congruence still holds. Notice that $7$ is the difference between the two congruences:\n$$ 43 - 43 + 7 \\equiv 4 + 7 \\pmod{6}$$\n$$ 40 \\equiv 11 \\pmod{6}$$\n$$ 4 \\equiv 5 \\pmod{6}$$\n\nThis implies:\n$$ n \\equiv 5 \\pmod{6} \\quad \\quad \\quad (5)$$\n\nNow, let's consider equation $(2)$ again and substitute $n$ with the smallest value that satisfies both $n \\equiv 4 \\pmod{6}$ and $n \\equiv 3 \\pmod{5}$. This leads us to:\n$$ n = 34 \\text{ Which is the smallest value that satisfies (1) and (2) } $$\n\nUltimately, we're asked to find the remainder when $n$ is divided by seven. We know that:\n$$ n \\equiv 5 \\pmod{6} \\quad \\quad \\quad (6)$$\n\nSubstituting equation $(6)$ into equation $(5)$, we proceed to solve for $n \\pmod{7}$:\n$$ n \\equiv 5 \\times 5 \\equiv 25 \\pmod{7} \\quad \\quad \\quad (7)$$\n\nFinally, $25$ modulo $7$ equals:\n$$ 25 \\equiv 4 \\pmod{7} \\quad \\quad \\quad (8)$$\n\nThus, the remainder when dividing $n$ by seven is $\\boxed{4}$.",
